faqs:
  - question: "What are MCP Events?"
    answer: "MCP Events is an extension to the Model Context Protocol that lets a client subscribe to things that happen on a server and hear about them when they occur. Instead of the agent polling with tool calls, the server pushes each event to the agent. The proposal is tracked as [SEP-3415](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3415) in the MCP specification repository."
  - question: "Which Appwrite events can an agent subscribe to?"
    answer: "Six events at launch: `users.user.created`, `functions.execution.failed`, `functions.deployment.completed`, `sites.deployment.completed`, `tablesdb.row.created`, and `storage.file.created`. Every event takes a `project_id`, and most also take the ID of the function, site, table, or bucket to watch."
  - question: "What is the difference between push and webhook delivery?"
    answer: "Push delivery (`events/stream`) is for agents running on your machine, such as Pi with the `pi-mcp-events` extension. The agent holds a request open and events arrive on it. Webhook delivery (`events/subscribe`) is for clients that have a public HTTPS endpoint: the server sends a signed POST to that endpoint for every event."
  - question: "Does the MCP server store my subscriptions?"
    answer: "No. Each subscription is one Appwrite [webhook](/docs/apis/webhooks) in your own project, created with your OAuth token. The subscription details are encrypted and stored on that webhook, so the MCP server keeps no database of subscriptions. You can see these webhooks in the Appwrite Console, and they are deleted when you unsubscribe or close the stream."
  - question: "What data does an event contain?"
    answer: "Only IDs and metadata, such as `user_id` and `created_at` for a new user. Event payloads never contain row contents, emails, phone numbers, or execution logs. The agent fetches any details it needs with a normal tool call, using the same permissions you granted at sign in."
  - question: "Do MCP Events count against my plan?"
    answer: "Each active subscription or open stream uses one webhook in the project. The Free plan allows 2 webhooks per project, and Pro allows unlimited webhooks. If you reach the limit, the server returns a clear error so the agent can tell you to upgrade or unsubscribe from another event."
---

Until now, an AI agent connected to the Appwrite MCP server could only act when you asked it to. It could create a user, deploy a function, or read a table, but it had no way of knowing that something had changed in your project since the last time it looked. If a function started failing at 2 AM, the agent found out when you did.

Today, we are announcing **MCP Events** for the [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers). Your agent can now subscribe to events in your Appwrite project, such as a new user signing up, a function execution failing, or a deployment finishing, and react the moment they happen.

In this demo, [Pi](https://pi.dev) subscribes to new users in an Appwrite project and goes idle. In a second terminal, Codex creates a user through the Appwrite MCP server. The event reaches Pi in about a second and wakes it up without a new prompt. Pi looks up the new user, reports back, and then unsubscribes.

